29	\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
30	\label{par:phase-segmentation}
31	Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Validation against DeepSeek's explicit \texttt{<think>} markers using adaptive per-sequence quantile segmentation yields substantial agreement ($\kappa{=}0.88$, AUC-ROC${=}0.98$; $n{=}48$; Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}). Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.
